1
00:00:03,636 --> 00:00:06,896
Start with the Architecture template.

2
00:00:06,946 --> 00:00:18,063
Draw a wall to hold the panel while we build it.

3
00:00:18,113 --> 00:00:24,404
Place axis helpers at the base,
one at each side of the panel.

4
00:00:24,454 --> 00:00:33,636
The Window tool gives us a frame and glass in two clicks.

5
00:00:33,686 --> 00:00:41,187
Good. Explode turns the window into
plain geometry that hotspots can drive.

6
00:00:41,237 --> 00:00:49,629
Delete the wall and clear the helpers,
and we're left with the panel.

7
00:00:49,679 --> 00:01:05,758
Make Object, then Edit Prototype.
Hotspots are defined inside the object.

8
00:01:05,808 --> 00:01:13,308
First the size handle. Place Hotspot on
the panel's right corner, set to Stretch.

9
00:01:13,358 --> 00:01:21,819
Now the multiplier: another hotspot on the grid
in front, named Count, set to Multiply Linear.

10
00:01:21,869 --> 00:01:30,058
Window-select the whole panel and
attach it. This is what gets copied.

11
00:01:30,108 --> 00:01:37,128
Pick Reference, one panel width back.
That distance is the step between copies.

12
00:01:37,178 --> 00:01:45,956
Back to Size: select the panel's right side
and the Count hotspot too, then attach them.

13
00:01:46,006 --> 00:01:51,428
Attaching Count means the copies will
follow when the panel gets wider.

14
00:01:51,478 --> 00:02:00,418
Nice. Finish Editing. That's the
whole definition, with no code at all.

15
00:02:00,468 --> 00:02:08,469
Select the panel, and both handles
appear. Drag Size to make it wider.

16
00:02:08,519 --> 00:02:16,959
Now pull back to see more, and drag Count
along the grid. Each panel width adds a panel.

17
00:02:17,009 --> 00:02:21,309
Wow! A whole curtain wall from one drag.

18
00:02:21,359 --> 00:02:33,912
Move Copy it, and each copy keeps its own size and count.

19
00:02:33,962 --> 00:02:42,361
Drag this one back to three
panels. The first doesn't change.

20
00:02:42,411 --> 00:02:51,371
That's a dynamic object with no code:
one size handle and one multiply handle.
